#!/usr/bin/env bash
#
# lane-start — the FIRST act of a lane: name the tmux window after the lane,
# put the lane's row in the register, and launch the session under the same
# name. One command at the boundary, so the three things that must agree are
# set in one act and cannot drift apart.
#
# THE LANE RULE (Brett Heap, 2026-09-10)
#   lane = tmux window name = Claude session name, in the form
#   `<repo>-<position across, left to right>`. The working directory confers
#   NO lane — two lanes may share one; a window still called `claude` has no
#   lane at all; and whether a name is free is decided by whether the
#   register row's recorded session id is LIVE, never by whether a row exists.
#
# PROTOCOL  ~/.agents/protocols/lane-collision-protocol.md
#   Rule 4 + Amendment 2 — the window is NAMED for the lane BEFORE the row is
#     appended, and peers are addressed by that name.
#   Rule 9 + Amendment 5 — every register write is one commit, made by
#     lanes-edit.sh, in the person's workspace repository.
#   Rule 10 — every row names its workstation.
#   Amendment 7 (DRAFT 2026-09-11) — after the row is written, the lane's own
#     object log records that the lane is here. Every line has an object, and
#     a lane-kind line's object is the lane itself:
#       STARTED — lane X, session <uuid>@<ws>, <UTC>, lane:X → home owner/repo; estate <estate>
#     `RESUMED` for a session that is being resumed rather than started. The
#     home repo comes from `git remote get-url origin` of the lane's OWN
#     directory: `<repo>-<n>` is a label, not a scope, and this line is the
#     only thing that says where a lane actually lives. It is resolved through
#     `lanes/repos.tsv` before it is written (R20), because a home is inherited
#     by every `#n` that lane writes and a legacy org spelling would key those
#     against a repository nothing else can reconcile them with.
#   Amendment 6 (DRAFT 2026-09-11, awaiting Brett Heap's word) — A LANE IS
#     RESUMED BY THE SESSION ID ITS ROW RECORDS, NEVER BY ITS TITLE. Titles are
#     not unique: `claude --help` says `-r, --resume [value]` is "Resume a
#     conversation by session ID, or open interactive picker with optional
#     search term", so only a session id is an exact resume — a title merely
#     filters the picker. On 2026-09-11 four transcripts in one working
#     directory carried the title `codeXfactory-1` and eight more carried
#     `codeXfactory-1 (2)` / `(4)`, while the session Brett was actually
#     driving carried no title at all.
#   Rule 3 + Amendment 8 (DRAFT 2026-09-12) — THE HANDOFF IS STAMPED HERE TOO.
#     On a resume, the handoff the row points at gets Rule 3's line, in place,
#     as the line after its header block:
#       RESUMED by <uuid> (lane <name>) at <UTC> — lane-start on <ws>
#     committed on its own as `handoff(<lane>@<ws>): RESUMED by <uuid>`, with
#     an explicit pathspec and a `Lane:` trailer (Amendment 4(d)), then pulled
#     and pushed. It NEVER refuses the launch: a stamp that cannot be written
#     prints the command that writes it. Seven resumes of one lane needed that
#     line written by hand, and while it was owed the counts drifted 6/4/5
#     across the row, the object log and the handoff itself.
#
# USAGE
#   lane-start [options] <repo> <n>      # lane-start openRepoShape 2
#   lane-start [options] <repo>-<n>      # the full lane name works too
#   lane-start [options] <n>             # <repo> = basename of the cwd's git root
#   lane-start --dir <path> <lane>       # any lane name, when --dir says where
#
# <repo> is parsed out of the lane name only because it is what locates the
# lane's directory. With `--dir` there is nothing left to parse, so the name is
# taken VERBATIM — which is how a lane named before the `<repo>-<n>` rule
# (`browser-ui-repair`) is started.
#
# OPTIONS
#   --dir <path>     the lane's checkout            (default $PROJECTS_ROOT/<repo>)
#   --estate <name>  the handoff estate folder      (default <repo>)
#   --dry-run        print every step; rename nothing, write nothing, launch
#                    nothing
#   --no-launch      do everything except the launch, and print the claude
#                    command on stdout — for the launcher integration and the
#                    tests
#   --confirm        when this window's name is not the lane, print the three
#                    facts that disagree — the WINDOW, the SESSION live in it,
#                    the ROW — and ask before taking it. The launcher passes it
#                    when the lane came from the swap RECORD rather than from
#                    the window name or a flag (Amendment 8)
#   --yes            answer that question yes without asking, for a caller that
#                    has already asked
#   -h, --help
#   --               everything after it is passed through to claude
#
# WHAT IT DOES, in order
#   1. derives LANE=<repo>-<n> and the lane's directory (which must exist);
#   2. refuses unless it is already inside tmux: it renames the window it is
#      run in and never creates a session;
#   3. LIVENESS CHECK, before it takes the name. If the register already has a
#      row for LANE it reads the session id(s) out of THE PUBLISHED row's
#      session cell — `lanes-edit.sh register-row`, which fetches and reads
#      `origin/<branch>` — and looks for a LIVE record (pid alive, start time
#      still matching, a status that is not `ended`) in every profile's
#      `sessions/*.json`. A live holder in ANOTHER tmux window is a refusal. A
#      row whose recorded sessions are all dead is a lane you are RESUMING, and
#      that is allowed;
#   3b. the LIVE SESSION IN THIS WINDOW (Amendment 8), which is not the same
#      question as step 3's. The harness mints a new transcript uuid with
#      nobody acting — a `/clear` does it, and so did the 2026-09-12 usage
#      reset that carried this lane's conversation into `4a91f1dc…` while its
#      row still ended on `09dd34d1…`. That id is in NO row, so step 3 cannot
#      see it and answers "this lane is parked" about the conversation sitting
#      in the very window it was asked about. Keyed on the WINDOW instead
#      (`lanes-edit.sh window-session`), the record is found: its uuid is
#      appended to the session cell in step 5, BEFORE the stamp, and it is what
#      this run resumes and stamps. There is no flag for it and there could not
#      be one — the id does not exist until the session does, so the act is
#      `lane-start --no-launch <repo> <n>` run IN that window, which is what the
#      SessionStart hook's block tells the new session to do;
#   3c. `--confirm`, if it was passed and this window is not already named for
#      the lane: the triple in front of a person, and a question. Before the
#      rename and before any write, so a No costs nothing;
#   4. `tmux rename-window <LANE>` — which also turns automatic-rename OFF for
#      that window (tmux(1) rename-window), so the name survives the next
#      command the window runs;
#   5. the row: `lanes-edit.sh add-row` when there is none, otherwise
#      `append-row-status`. Never a hand edit and never a whole-file write.
#      THE STATUS IS AMENDMENT 6(c)'s STAMP, written here rather than owed to
#      the session afterwards:
#        <UTC> RESUMED by <uuid> (lane <lane>) — lane-start on <ws>: window
#        renamed, launching <the command>
#      The TAIL states what happened, so a `--no-launch` run ends `window
#      renamed, no launch` and never claims to have launched anything.
#      `STARTED` where a new session takes the name, and the uuid is the one
#      this run actually resumes or mints, read from the PUBLISHED row. A stamp
#      that is owed is a stamp that is missed, and under Amendment 6(b) a missed
#      stamp is not bookkeeping debt — it is a lost lane. A brand-new row needs
#      no stamp: it is created with that uuid already in its session cell;
#   6. the launch. It is DECIDED in step 5a, before the row is written, because
#      a brand-new session's id is minted there and step 5 must record it.
#      RESUME BEATS NEW throughout — a rename into a title that is still held
#      mints `<LANE> (2)` and the lane loses its address. Three cases, in order:
#        a. `exec claude --resume <id>`, where <id> is THE LAST session id in
#           the PUBLISHED row's session cell and
#           `<projects state>/<cwd key>/<id>.jsonl` exists. Exact: that
#           conversation and no other.
#        b. `exec claude --resume <LANE>` when no recorded id resolves but a
#           transcript here carries the custom title <LANE> — or `<LANE> (N)`,
#           which is the same lane: that suffix is what a rename into a title
#           something else still holds mints, and the liveness check has always
#           read it that way. THE FALLBACK, not the rule — it opens the picker
#           filtered by the title, and several transcripts can share one title.
#           A suffix found here is reported: it means a stale holder was live,
#           and the cure is to retire the holders, not to live with `(4)`.
#        c. `exec claude --name <LANE> --session-id <fresh uuid>` for a lane
#           with no history here. The uuid is minted so the row can carry it,
#           which is what makes (a) work on the next start.
#
# THE RESUME TARGET — WHY THE LAST ID, AND WHY THE PUBLISHED CELL
#   The session cell is a HISTORY, written left to right: a lane's row reads
#   `<first> → <after the first /clear> → <after the profile switch>`, oldest
#   first, because each succeeding session appends itself. So the LAST session
#   id in the cell is the lane's current one, and that is the only id this
#   script will resume. Earlier ids are the lane's past and are never resumed
#   (a stale lineage is precisely what went wrong on 2026-09-11).
#
#   AND THE CELL IS THE PUBLISHED ONE. `$LANES_FILE` is this checkout's working
#   tree, and a `git fetch` does not move a working tree: a uuid a peer stamped
#   from another clone and pushed is simply absent here until something pulls.
#   Read from there, a row published as `<U1> → <U2>` still reads `<U1>` — so
#   the operator lands in the lane's PREVIOUS conversation, the status appended
#   to the row names the wrong id, and the RESUMED line goes into
#   `lanes/log/<lane>.md` carrying a session uuid that is not the lane's
#   current one, in a file nothing ever rewrites. Every other state read in
#   this tooling goes through `origin/<branch>` after a fetch; this one does
#   too, through the same `register-row` this script already asks before it
#   adds a row. `live-holder`'s id set stays a UNION of both (R25) and is the
#   one exception, for the opposite reason: liveness fails CLOSED, so an id
#   only this checkout knows is one more reason to refuse a rename.
#
# KEEPING THE ROW CURRENT
#   Resuming by a recorded id only works if the row is current, so this script
#   makes it current where it can:
#     * case (c), NEW ROW — the row is created with the minted id already in
#       its session cell, instead of `pending — set by the session's first act`;
#     * case (c), EXISTING ROW — `lanes-edit.sh append-session-id` appends
#       `→ harness <minted id>` after the cell's last id, matching that id
#       INSIDE THE SESSION CELL: the 6(c) stamp writes the same uuid twice more
#       into the state cell, so a whole-row anchor (`replace-in-row`) refuses on
#       every row this tool has already stamped — three occurrences in this
#       estate's own `openRepoProject-1` row. When the cell has no
#       id to append to (some rows record only `session_…` footer ids, which
#       are not transcript ids), it prints the command and leaves the act to
#       the session — it never guesses at the cell's text;
#     * case (a) — nothing to do: the row already names the session being
#       resumed;
#     * case (b) — the id cannot be known before the picker runs, so the
#       session stamps its own row as its first act (Amendment 6(c)).
#   `--session-id` is set to LANE_START_SESSION_ID=0 to turn the minting off;
#   the row then reads `pending — set by the session's first act` as before.
#
# WHAT COUNTS AS A SESSION ID IN THE CELL
#   The TRANSCRIPT uuid — the name of the session's `<uuid>.jsonl` — and only
#   that, because it is the only id `--resume` takes and the only one the
#   liveness records key on. Some rows record a PR-FOOTER id (`session_01…`)
#   instead; those cannot be resumed or checked, so the script names them and
#   says the row needs the transcript uuid rather than treating the cell as
#   empty. Every RESUMED / handoff / takeover stamp writes the transcript uuid.
#
# OUTPUT  every step and every refusal goes to stderr; the ONLY thing written
#   to stdout is `--no-launch`'s command line, so `--no-launch` composes.
#
# EXIT
#   0  done
#   1  environment — not inside tmux, no tmux, a RECORDED directory that is
#      gone, no register, or `lanes-edit.sh live-holder` failed (which is NOT
#      the same as its answering 8, "no live session holds this lane")
#   2  refusal — an ARGUMENT ERROR, and one further case this amendment adds: a
#      name that is not a lane, a position that is not a position, a name a
#      window cannot take, a lane whose register holds two rows and so resolves
#      to none, a lane that is live in another window — and A LANE WHOSE
#      CHECKOUT IS NOT RECORDED AND CANNOT BE PROVED (Evidence 7).
#
#      THAT LAST ONE MOVED FROM 1 TO 2 AND THE MOVE IS THE POINT. Evidence 7:
#      a lane whose record predates clause (c) carries no `dir`, the default
#      `$PROJECTS_ROOT/<repo>` did not exist, and this script exited **1**
#      behind the launcher's `exec` — a pane that said `[exited]` with the
#      message scrolled past it. It is a REFUSAL a person fixes with one flag,
#      not an environment that failed, and `restart` already spends 2 on the
#      same fact (clause (i), `R-A11-20`: *"no such lane, no recorded
#      directory, no profile recorded yet"*). A directory the lane itself
#      RECORDED and which is now gone keeps 1: that is a path that was right
#      and moved, and nothing is searched for in its place.
#
#      A PERSON'S ANSWER IS NOT AN ARGUMENT ERROR. `--confirm` answered `N`, and
#      `--confirm` with no tty and no `--yes` — the same answer with nobody
#      there to give it — rename nothing, write nothing, print one notice and
#      `exec` Claude BARE with the pass-through arguments, exit 0. A launcher
#      that `exec`s this script would otherwise leave the operator in an exited
#      pane with no Claude in it. Under `--no-launch` the bare command is
#      printed on stdout, so a launcher can exec it itself.
#
# ENVIRONMENT
#   PROJECTS_ROOT        default ~/projects
#   LANES_FILE           default <the workspace repository>/lanes/LANES.md
#   LANES_EDIT           default lanes-edit.sh beside this command
#   AGENT_PROTOCOL_ROOT  dir holding workspace.yaml (default ~/.agents)
#   OPENREPOTOOLS_BIN_DIR  where `--install` put the commands (default
#                        ~/.local/bin) — searched for lanes-edit.sh
#   CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR    the profile whose sessions/ and projects/ are read
#   CLAUDE_PROJECTS_DIR  default $CLAUDE_CONFIG_DIR/projects, else ~/.claude/projects
#   CLAUDE_BIN           default `claude` from PATH
#   LANE_START_SESSION_ID  0 turns off minting a uuid for a new session (and
#                        therefore turns off writing it into the row)
#   LANE_START_ANSWER    answers `--confirm`'s question without a terminal —
#                        the seam the tests drive it through. Set at all, it is
#                        the answer; anything but y/yes is No
#   LANES_REPO           the checkout the register and the handoffs share
#                        (default: the workspace repository)
#   LANES_WORKSPACE_ROOT the workspace repository, overriding the `path:` in
#                        ${AGENT_PROTOCOL_ROOT:-~/.agents}/workspace.yaml — the
#                        test seam, and the only override there is
#   LANES_BRANCH         default main — the branch the handoff stamp is pushed on
#   LANES_GIT_TIMEOUT    seconds bounding each git call that touches the NETWORK
#                        (default 60; 0 or a non-number turns the bound off)
#   LANES_GIT            the git binary THOSE calls use, so a test can hand them
#                        a git that hangs without shadowing git for the script
#   LANES_NO_GIT=1       write the handoff's stamp but commit nothing
#
# It spells no absolute path of its own. Since Amendment 9(a) the register is
# found through `$AGENT_PROTOCOL_ROOT/workspace.yaml` rather than beside this
# file — this is an installed command now, and a command's own directory is
# evidence about the command, not about a person's data. Its WRITER is found
# beside it, because `--install` places the two together. Everything else hangs
# off $HOME.

set -euo pipefail

prog=lane-start

SELF="${BASH_SOURCE[0]}"
RESOLVED="$(readlink -f -- "$SELF" 2>/dev/null || printf '%s' "$SELF")"
SCRIPT_DIR="$(cd -- "$(dirname -- "$RESOLVED")" && pwd)"

# --- THE WORKSPACE REPOSITORY (lane-collision-protocol Amendment 9(a)) -------
#
# The person's data — the register `lanes/LANES.md`, the object logs
# `lanes/log/`, the handoffs, the manifests `park` writes, and a
# `lanes/repos.tsv` override — is found through
# `$AGENT_PROTOCOL_ROOT/workspace.yaml`, and NEVER from this file's own
# location. `resume:466` and `status:1673` already read that same file under
# that same name, and Amendment 9(a) mints no second one: a second way to find
# it is a second answer.
#
# Deriving it from here stopped being POSSIBLE the moment this became an
# installed regular file in `~/.local/bin` with no register anywhere near it,
# and it stopped being WANTED for a subtler reason: a file's location on disk
# is evidence about the file, not about a person's data.
#
# THESE FOUR FUNCTIONS ARE A DELIBERATE COPY, byte for byte, across
# `lanes-edit.sh`, `lane-start`, `lane-end` and `link-estates` — the same trade
# openRepoTools already makes for its own fetch shim (`openRepoTools:93-100`).
# A shared `lanes-common.sh` would be a TENTH file for `--install` to place and
# a broken helper the first time somebody copied only some of them. Each of
# these is one file a person has on PATH.
#
# FAILING TO FIND IT IS A REFUSAL, NEVER A GUESS. No file, no `path:`, no
# `repository:`, or a `path:` that is not the root of a checkout of the
# `repository:` named beside it → the caller refuses, exit 1 — the code
# `lanes-edit.sh` has always used for *registry not found* — with the fix
# named: `openRepoTools wip init`. Nothing here ever creates that file, writes
# a register, or falls back to a path derived from its own location. THE
# CHECKOUT TEST IS NEW: nothing validated it before this, so a `path:` pointing
# at some other repository used to be accepted and now is not.
#
# `LANES_WORKSPACE_ROOT` is the test seam and the only override — the seam
# `lane-start` already spelled for handoff resolution, widened to the whole
# question so `test_lane_helpers.sh` can point every helper at one sandbox.
# --- BEGIN shared workspace resolver (Amendment 9(a)) ----------------------
# BYTE-IDENTICAL IN ALL FOUR LANE HELPERS, and held there by
# `tests/test_repo_hygiene.py`. The estate commands carry the same discipline
# for their own estate resolver and for the same reason its guard gives: a
# drifted copy is TWO ANSWERS to one question, and "where is this person's
# workspace" is exactly the question Amendment 9(a) exists to give one answer
# to. Edit it in one file and the test names the other three.
LANES_WS_WHY=""

lanes_ws_yaml() { printf '%s\n' "${AGENT_PROTOCOL_ROOT:-$HOME/.agents}/workspace.yaml"; }

# `key: value` at the TOP LEVEL only, first occurrence, with an inline `#`
# comment and surrounding quotes stripped and a leading `~` expanded against
# $HOME (nothing expands a bare `~` inside a file). The anchored `^key:` never
# reaches into the INDENTED `orgs:` map, which Amendment 9(a) puts out of
# scope: these helpers read `repository:` and `path:` and nothing else, because
# one register in the first workspace repository is the whole rule — the
# register never follows that map. Two lines of sed, because a YAML parser is
# not a dependency any of these files will take for one scalar.
lanes_ws_field() {
  local v
  v="$(sed -n -e "s/^$1:[[:space:]]*//p" "$2" 2>/dev/null | head -n 1 |
    sed -e 's/[[:space:]]*#.*$//' -e 's/[[:space:]]*$//')"
  v="${v%\"}"; v="${v#\"}"
  v="${v%\'}"; v="${v#\'}"
  case "$v" in
  '~') v="$HOME" ;;
  '~/'*) v="$HOME/${v#'~/'}" ;;
  esac
  printf '%s\n' "$v"
}

# THE WORKSTATION'S NAME IS READ, NEVER INVENTED — AND NEVER `hostname` INSIDE A
# CONTAINER (lane-collision-protocol Amendment 11, ratified decision 8(d), as
# corrected by A11 Addendum 3's `R-A11-14`).
#
# WHAT WENT WRONG. `opensoft/brett-wip` `origin/main` carries SIX lines in
# `lanes/log/openRepoProject-1.md` whose workstation is a container id — five
# `@0e7d1a79a07e`, one `@55cceb3e5bc9` — in an append-only file, written by every
# writer inside that container and not only by Evidence 6's fork. A row on a
# workstation that does not exist is a row no reader can match, no crossing can
# detect and no `swapped <ws>` can answer for; and the log is never rewritten, so
# each one is wrong for ever.
#
# WHO OWNS THE VALUE. `R-A11-14`: the **workBenches launcher**, which runs on the
# host and exports `LANES_WORKSTATION=<the host's own hostname -s>` into every
# session AND every container it starts, beside the `CLAUDE_PROFILE_NAME` it
# already exports. These helpers READ it. They do not write it, they do not
# derive it from a second file, and THEY NEVER SUBSTITUTE A PLACEHOLDER: an
# `@unknown-workstation` is the same defect one field along from the `unknown`
# clause (e) refuses in the session field, and the rejected alternative — a
# `workstation:` key in `workspace.yaml` — is a SECOND PLACE FOR THE TRUTH TO BE
# WRONG beside the variable the launcher already sets.
#
# THE THREE ANSWERS, as `<name><TAB><source>`:
#
#   seam              `$LANES_WORKSTATION` is set. This is the ordinary case
#                     wherever the launcher started the session.
#   hostname          no seam, and this is NOT a container: `hostname -s`, which
#                     is what the seam has defaulted to since Amendment 6 and
#                     what every row written before this rung was written by.
#   container-unset   no seam, and this IS a container. The NAME still answers,
#                     because a read in front of every launch may not refuse —
#                     it will simply match nothing, which is the honest answer —
#                     but every WRITER refuses, exit 2, naming the variable and
#                     the launcher that sets it.
#
# IT ANSWERS AS A PAIR, AND THAT IS NOT A STYLE CHOICE. A caller reads the name
# with `$(lanes_workstation)`, which is a COMMAND SUBSTITUTION and therefore a
# subshell: a global the function set there dies with it. The source is on the
# same line as the name, which is the only way out of a subshell there is.
#
#   $LANES_IN_CONTAINER   1 or 0, forcing the probe. THE TEST SEAM, and nothing
#                         else sets it: the suite must be able to exercise both
#                         halves of this rule on one host, and the host it runs
#                         on is a container exactly when it is.
lanes_in_container() {
  case "${LANES_IN_CONTAINER:-}" in
  1) return 0 ;;
  0) return 1 ;;
  esac
  [ -e /.dockerenv ] || [ -e /run/.containerenv ]
}

lanes_workstation_pair() {
  local v
  if [ -n "${LANES_WORKSTATION:-}" ]; then
    printf '%s\t%s\n' "$LANES_WORKSTATION" seam
    return 0
  fi
  v="$(hostname -s 2>/dev/null || hostname 2>/dev/null || printf 'unknown')"
  if lanes_in_container; then
    printf '%s\t%s\n' "$v" container-unset
  else
    printf '%s\t%s\n' "$v" hostname
  fi
}

# THE NAME ALONE, which is what every writer of the workstation column wants.
lanes_workstation() { lanes_workstation_pair | cut -f1; }

# The ONE SENTENCE a writer refuses with, in one place, so the three writers
# cannot word it three ways. Empty on every other source, so
# `[ -n "$(lanes_workstation_why "$src")" ]` is the whole of the test.
lanes_workstation_why() {   # <source>
  [ "${1:-}" = container-unset ] || return 0
  printf '%s\n' "this is a container and \$LANES_WORKSTATION is not set, so there is no workstation name to write. This host's own \`hostname\` is the CONTAINER'S id, not a workstation, and a row on a workstation that does not exist is a row no reader can match — in a log nothing ever rewrites (Amendment 11, decision 8(d); R-A11-14). The value is the workBenches launcher's to export into every session and container it starts. Set it for this one and re-run: export LANES_WORKSTATION=<this host's name>"
}

# One of the spellings `git remote get-url origin` prints for one GitHub
# repository, reduced to a bare lowercased `<owner>/<name>` so it can be
# compared against workspace.yaml's own `<owner>/<name>`. A URL that is not
# GitHub's is compared as it stands, lowercased — which is what lets a sandbox
# name a bare local origin and still be checked.
lanes_ws_norm() {
  local u="${1%.git}"
  case "$u" in
  https://github.com/*) u="${u#https://github.com/}" ;;
  ssh://git@github.com/*) u="${u#ssh://git@github.com/}" ;;
  git@github.com:*) u="${u#git@github.com:}" ;;
  esac
  printf '%s' "$u" | tr '[:upper:]' '[:lower:]'
}

# Do these two name one repository? Equal after normalisation, OR the origin's
# path ENDS in the `<owner>/<name>` the pointer file spells — which is how a
# GitHub Enterprise host, an ssh alias and a local bare mirror laid out by
# owner and name all say the same thing as `https://github.com/<owner>/<name>`.
# The pointer file spells `<owner>/<name>` because that is what a person types;
# `git remote get-url` spells a URL because that is what git stores.
lanes_ws_same_repo() {
  local a b
  a="$(lanes_ws_norm "$1")"
  b="$(lanes_ws_norm "$2")"
  [ -n "$a" ] && [ -n "$b" ] || return 1
  [ "$a" = "$b" ] && return 0
  case "$a" in */"$b") return 0 ;; esac
  return 1
}

# Prints the workspace checkout's real root, or returns 1 having set
# $LANES_WS_WHY to the one sentence that says which of the five ways it failed.
# ROOT-NESS IS ITS OWN CHECK: `rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree` and
# `remote get-url origin` both succeed from any SUBDIRECTORY of a work tree, so
# a `path:` naming a subdirectory would otherwise be accepted and resolve
# `<path>/lanes/LANES.md` inside the wrong directory.
lanes_workspace_root() {
  local yaml repo path origin toplevel real
  LANES_WS_WHY=""
  if [ -n "${LANES_WORKSPACE_ROOT:-}" ]; then
    printf '%s\n' "$LANES_WORKSPACE_ROOT"
    return 0
  fi
  yaml="$(lanes_ws_yaml)"
  if [ ! -r "$yaml" ]; then
    LANES_WS_WHY="there is no $yaml"
    return 1
  fi
  repo="$(lanes_ws_field repository "$yaml")"
  path="$(lanes_ws_field path "$yaml")"
  if [ -z "$repo" ]; then
    LANES_WS_WHY="$yaml names no \`repository:\`"
    return 1
  fi
  if [ -z "$path" ]; then
    LANES_WS_WHY="$yaml names no \`path:\`"
    return 1
  fi
  if ! git -C "$path" rev-parse --is-inside-work-tree >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    LANES_WS_WHY="$yaml names path: $path, which is not a git checkout"
    return 1
  fi
  origin="$(git -C "$path" remote get-url origin 2>/dev/null || :)"
  if ! lanes_ws_same_repo "$origin" "$repo"; then
    LANES_WS_WHY="$yaml names repository: $repo, but $path is a checkout of ${origin:-no origin at all}"
    return 1
  fi
  toplevel="$(git -C "$path" rev-parse --show-toplevel 2>/dev/null || :)"
  real="$(cd -- "$path" 2>/dev/null && pwd -P)" || real=""
  if [ -z "$real" ] || [ "$toplevel" != "$real" ]; then
    LANES_WS_WHY="$yaml names path: $path, which is not the ROOT of that checkout (${toplevel:-unknown} is)"
    return 1
  fi
  printf '%s\n' "$real"
}

# The refusal's body, so all four helpers say the same thing in the same words.
#
# THE REASON IS RE-DERIVED HERE, IN THE PARENT SHELL, and that is the whole
# point of this line. Every caller reaches the resolver through a command
# substitution — `LANES_WS_ROOT="$(lanes_workspace_root || :)"` — which runs it
# in a SUBSHELL, so the $LANES_WS_WHY it set died with that subshell and the
# parent still holds the empty string it started with. Six distinct diagnoses
# were being computed and all six were being thrown away; every one of the
# refusals Amendment 9(a) enumerates printed the same "no reason recorded".
# The resolver is read-only and cheap — two `sed`s and three `git` reads — and
# it is reached on the refusal path only, so running it again HERE is the one
# place its answer survives to be printed.
lanes_workspace_why() {
  [ -n "$LANES_WS_WHY" ] || lanes_workspace_root >/dev/null 2>&1 || :
  printf '%s' "the workspace repository could not be found — ${LANES_WS_WHY:-no reason recorded}.
    Amendment 9(a): every lane helper reads \`repository:\` and \`path:\` from
    $(lanes_ws_yaml), never from its own location. One command writes that
    file, creates or clones the repository and places the symlinks:
        openRepoTools wip init"
}
